  ---
  What the Pi does automatically (you don't touch this)

  Every 5 minutes, the Pi quietly runs a check in the background:
  - Asks GitHub: "is there anything new on the main branch?"
  - If no: does nothing, goes back to sleep
  - If yes:
    a. Stops the RF monitor app
    b. Downloads the new code (git pull)
    c. Builds it (dotnet build)
    d. Starts the app again with the new code

  If the build fails for some reason (broken code was pushed), the Pi restarts on the previous working version rather than going dark.

  ---
  Checking what's happening on the Pi (useful for debugging)

  Open a Terminal on the Pi and use these commands:

  Is the app running?
  sudo systemctl status rf-monitor
  Look for the word active (running) in green. If it says failed, the app crashed.

  See the live output of the app (what you'd normally see in the terminal when running manually):
  sudo journalctl -u rf-monitor -f
  Press Ctrl+C to stop watching.

  Did an automatic update happen? Did it succeed?
  sudo journalctl -u rf-monitor-updater -f
  You'll see timestamped lines like Up to date (nothing to do) or Update detected: abc123 → def456 followed by the build and restart
  steps.

  Force an update right now (don't wait 5 minutes):
  sudo bash ~/rf-monitor/RtlSdrTest/scripts/pi-update.sh

  Restart the app manually (if it crashed or you stopped it):
  sudo systemctl start rf-monitor

  Stop the app (if you want to do manual testing):
  sudo systemctl stop rf-monitor

